This item tests your reasoning. Tracing this code:
int[] a = {3, 5, 7, 10, 12, 17, 18, 23, 26};
int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1, steps = 0;
while (lo <= hi) { int mid = (lo + hi) / 2; steps++; if (a[mid] == 12) return steps; if (a[mid] < 12) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1; }
return steps;

In this exercise, answer the following. What value does this procedure return?
int[] a = {3, -3, -3, -4, 9, 7};
int acc = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) acc += a[i];
return acc;

Apply the relevant principle here. What does this string routine return?
String s = "izcxdia";
StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) out.append(s.charAt(i));
return out.toString();

Determine the outcome of this case. Which index pair does this scan return?
int[] a = {-1, 1, 4, 7, 10, 14, 15, 16};
int i = 0, j = a.length - 1;
while (i < j) { int s = a[i] + a[j]; if (s == 22) return new int[]{i, j}; if (s < 22) i++; else j--; }
return new int[]{-1, -1};

Work out what the setup implies. What does this pointer code return?
Node a = new Node(30);
Node b = new Node(20);
Node c = new Node(35);
a.next = b; b.next = c; c.next = null;
Node p = a;
while (p.next != null) p = p.next;
return p.value;

